  var easy_props_import_default = `@tool
extends EditorScenePostImport

# easy-props paints a prop's colours on its points. From Godot 4.7, a GLB's materials
# import with those colours turned off, so the prop comes in white. This turns them back
# on for every surface that has them. Godot runs it again on each reimport.


func _post_import(scene: Node) -> Object:
	for node in scene.find_children("*", "MeshInstance3D", true, false):
		var mesh: Mesh = (node as MeshInstance3D).mesh

		if mesh == null:
			continue

		for surface in mesh.get_surface_count():
			var material := mesh.surface_get_material(surface)

			if material is BaseMaterial3D and mesh.surface_get_format(surface) & Mesh.ARRAY_FORMAT_COLOR:
				(material as BaseMaterial3D).vertex_color_use_as_albedo = true

	return scene
`;

  // raw:frontend/engine-kits/godot/README.md
  var README_default = "# easy-props in Godot\n\nExport the prop with **Engine: Godot** and **Wind data** on.\n\n## Import\n\n1. Put the exported folder in your project. With **Materials** on when you export, the\n   `.gdshader` files for your look and `easy_props_import.gd` are already in it. Otherwise,\n   add them from this kit.\n2. Set this up once for the whole project, so every prop imports right, now and later. Open\n   **Project > Project Settings > Import Defaults** and choose **Scene**. Set **Import Script >\n   Path** to `easy_props_import.gd`, untick **Meshes > Generate LODs**, and press **Save**.\n   Then select the exported files, open the **Import** tab, and press **Reimport**.\n   - From Godot 4.7, props come in white without the import script: Godot leaves off the\n     colours easy-props paints on them. The script turns them back on, each time the file\n     is imported. Godot 4.6 and earlier don't need it, but it does no harm.\n   - Godot's automatic detail levels delete leaves, so a tree looks bare from a little way\n     off. Use the levels in the export instead.\n   - Make sure **Meshes > Light Baking** is not **Static Lightmaps**, which replaces the\n     wind data.\n3. Double-click the file and open **Advanced Import**, then the **Materials** tab.\n   Note the names ending in `_wood` and `_leaves`.\n\n## Make the materials\n\n1. In the FileSystem panel, right-click and choose **Create New > Resource > ShaderMaterial**.\n   Save it as `wood.tres`. Make another called `leaves.tres`.\n2. Open `wood.tres` and set **Shader** to `wood.gdshader`.\n3. Under **Shader Parameters**, use the images the import made beside the file: set **Bark\n   Texture** to the one ending in `_LOD0_0.png` and **Relief Texture** to `_LOD0_1.png`.\n4. Open `leaves.tres` and set **Shader** to `leaves.gdshader`.\n5. Under **Shader Parameters**, set **Dab Texture** to the image ending in `_LOD0_2.png`.\n   With the wrong one, the leaves show as solid polygons.\n\nFor a tree of a different kind, the numbers can differ. Open the image to check it: bark is\npale, relief is blue-purple, and leaves are scattered dabs.\n\n## Use them\n\n1. Drop the `_LOD0` file into a scene and select the node.\n2. Right-click it and tick **Editable Children**.\n3. Select each mesh and set **Surface Material Override > 0** to the matching material.\n\nIf you exported with **Join parts**, there is one mesh with a surface for each material.\nSet **Surface Material Override > 0** and **> 1**, matching the names you noted above.\n\n## Change the look\n\nThe settings are under **Shader Parameters**. Give both materials the same sway\nsettings, or the leaves slide away from the branches.\n\n| Setting | Does |\n|---|---|\n| Relief Strength | How deeply the bark looks carved |\n| Sway Strength | How far the whole tree bends, in metres at the tips |\n| Sway Speed | How fast it bends |\n| Flutter Strength | How far each leaf moves |\n| Flutter Speed | How fast the leaves shimmer |\n| Wind Direction | Which way the wind blows |\n\n## Cartoon and Comic looks\n\nThe kit also has toon materials. They move in the wind as the ones above do.\n\n| Look | Wood | Leaves |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cartoon: light in soft bands | `wood_cartoon.gdshader` | `leaves_cartoon.gdshader` |\n| Comic: flat colours and hard, deep shadows | `wood_comic.gdshader` | `leaves_comic.gdshader` |\n\nFor Comic, export with **Look: Comic**. That makes the colours in the file flat and turns the\nleaves' light to follow the round shape of each clump, so a crown shades in one piece. Cartoon\nworks with props exported in any look.\n\nMake the materials as above, with the toon shader in place of `wood.gdshader` and\n`leaves.gdshader`. They take the same textures. The Comic wood material leaves the relief\ntexture out, so you can skip it.\n\nComic is made for one sun, a **DirectionalLight3D** with shadows on. Other lights add light but\nno shadow colour. The scene's ambient light lifts the shadows, so keep it low: in your\n**WorldEnvironment**, set **Ambient Light > Energy** to about 0.3. **Colour Boost** and **Shadow\nDarkness** under **Shader Parameters** tune the colours.\n\n## Outlines\n\n`outline.gdshader` draws dark lines around shapes over the whole picture. It needs the\n**Forward+** renderer, which is Godot's default for desktop.\n\n1. Select your **Camera3D** and add a **MeshInstance3D** as its child.\n2. Set its **Mesh** to a new **QuadMesh**, and the QuadMesh's **Size** to 2 by 2.\n3. Under **Geometry**, set **Extra Cull Margin** to the largest value it takes, so the lines\n   never switch off.\n4. Set **Material Override** to a new **ShaderMaterial** with `outline.gdshader`.\n\n| Setting | Does |\n|---|---|\n| Line Width | Width in pixels. 3 for Comic, 1.5 for Cartoon |\n| Line Strength | How dark. 1 for Comic, 0.45 for Cartoon |\n| Depth Gap | How far behind a surface must be to get a line. Raise it if leaf crowns get speckled |\n| Gap Size | Gaps among leaves narrower than this go without a line |\n\nThe lines are found from how far away each point is, so a crown gets a few more small marks\nthan in the app, and the far edge of a ground that ends against the sky gets a line too.\n";

  // raw:frontend/engine-kits/godot/wood.gdshader
  var wood_default = "// easy-props wood. Paint comes from the vertex colours and the bark texture,\n// fine detail from the relief texture, and movement from UV2.\n// UV2.x is sway, from 0 at the base to 1 at the far tips.\nshader_type spatial;\n\nuniform sampler2D bark_texture : source_color, hint_default_white, repeat_enable, filter_linear_mipmap_anisotropic;\nuniform sampler2D relief_texture : hint_normal, repeat_enable, filter_linear_mipmap_anisotropic;\nuniform float relief_strength = 1.0;\nuniform float sway_strength = 0.25;\nuniform float sway_speed = 1.1;\nuniform vec3 wind_direction = vec3(1.0, 0.0, 0.3);\n\nvoid vertex() {\n	vec3 world = (MODEL_MATRIX * vec4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0)).xyz;\n	float phase = world.x * 0.37 + world.z * 0.53;\n	float wave = sin(TIME * sway_speed + phase) + 0.4 * sin(TIME * sway_speed * 2.3 + phase);\n	VERTEX += (inverse(MODEL_MATRIX) * vec4(normalize(wind_direction), 0.0)).xyz * wave * UV2.x * sway_strength;\n}\n\nvoid fragment() {\n	ALBEDO = COLOR.rgb * texture(bark_texture, UV).rgb;\n	NORMAL_MAP = texture(relief_texture, UV).rgb;\n	NORMAL_MAP_DEPTH = relief_strength;\n	ROUGHNESS = 0.85;\n}\n";

  // raw:frontend/engine-kits/unreal/README.md
  var README_default2 = '# Wind in Unreal\n\nExport the prop with **Engine: Unreal** and **Wind data** on. An Unreal material is a\nbinary file, so you build this one yourself, once per project. Tested on Unreal Engine\n5.8 with one tree.\n\nThe file stores movement in its second UV set: **UV1 red (R) is sway** and **UV1 green\n(G) is flutter**.\n\n## The quick way\n\nTurn on **Join parts** when you export, so each prop is one mesh you can place with the\nFoliage tool or PCG. Then, in the **Output Log**, switch the box at the bottom to **Cmd**\nand run:\n\n    py "C:/path/to/setup_props.py" "C:/path/to/export/folder"\n\nThis needs Unreal\'s Python Editor Script Plugin. It imports every prop in the folder to\n`/Game/EasyProps`, builds the materials below once, and gives each prop its own material\ninstances. It imports the full-detail version only, so add lighter levels yourself if you\nturn Nanite off. It also works without Join parts.\n\nThe rest of this guide does the same by hand.\n\n## Import\n\n1. Import the `_LOD0` file into its own folder.\n2. Keep **Recompute Normals** and **Generate Lightmap UVs** off.\n3. Unreal wraps each mesh in a large collision cage, which feels like an invisible wall.\n   For each tree, open the `_leaves` mesh and the `_wood` mesh and choose\n   **Collision > Remove Collision**. On the wood, add\n   **Collision > Add Capsule Simplified Collision** and fit it to the trunk.\n\n## Build the wind\n\nMake a new **Material Function** called `MF_PropWind`. Close any material tab before a\nscript or tool edits the same asset, because saving the tab can overwrite those changes.\n\n1. Connect **Object Position** and a **Constant3Vector** `0.001, 0.0015, 0` to a **Dot**, and **Add** a **Per Instance Random** to it. This keeps trees out of step.\n2. First wave: multiply **Time** by `0.2`, **Add** that offset, and connect the result to a **Sine**.\n3. Second wave: multiply **Time** by `0.46`, **Add** that offset, connect the result to a **Sine**, and multiply it by `0.4`.\n4. **Add** the two waves.\n5. Add **TextureCoordinate** with **Coordinate Index 1**. Multiply the waves by its **Mask (R)**, which holds the base of the tree still.\n6. Multiply by a **Constant3Vector** `25, 8, 0`. That is the sway.\n7. For flutter, connect **Absolute World Position** and a **Constant3Vector** `1, 1, 1` to a **Dot**, and divide it by `60`.\n8. **Add** that to **Time** times `0.8`, and connect the result to a **Sine**.\n9. Multiply by the **Mask (G)** of the same TextureCoordinate, by `1.5`, and by **Vertex Normal WS**.\n10. **Add** the sway and the flutter, and connect the sum to the **Output**.\n\nSizes are in centimetres. In a wood material the G channel is 0, so it never flutters.\n\n## Make the two materials\n\nWood, named `M_PropWood`:\n\n1. Add a **Texture Sample** using the imported bark texture. Multiply its **RGB** by **Vertex Color**, and connect that to **Base Color**.\n2. Add a second **Texture Sample** using the imported relief texture, and connect it to **Normal**.\n3. Add a constant 0.85 to **Roughness**.\n4. Connect `MF_PropWind` to **World Position Offset**.\n\nLeaves, named `M_PropLeaves`:\n\n1. Set **Blend Mode** to **Masked** and **Shading Model** to **Two Sided Foliage**. Leave **Two Sided** off: the leaf cards already have both faces, and turning it on spoils their soft shading.\n2. Add a **Texture Sample** using the imported leaf texture, the one of scattered dabs.\n3. Multiply its **RGB** by **Vertex Color**, and connect that to **Base Color**.\n4. Multiply the same result by 0.5, and connect that to **Subsurface Color**.\n5. Connect its **A** to **Opacity Mask**.\n6. Add a constant 1 to **Roughness**.\n7. Connect `MF_PropWind` to **World Position Offset**.\n\n## Use them\n\nAssign `M_PropWood` to the `_wood` mesh and `M_PropLeaves` to the `_leaves` mesh, and give\nboth the same **Location**. Each tree has its own leaf texture, so duplicate `M_PropLeaves`\nfor each new tree. Trees sway in the editor if **Realtime** is on in the viewport.\n\n## Cartoon and Comic looks\n\nToon materials for Unreal are not in the kit yet. You can already export with **Look: Comic**:\nthe colours in the file are flat, and the leaves\' light follows the round shape of each clump,\nready for a toon material of your own.\n';

